## Why This Is Asked
Advanced cross-DC TLS and policy debugging is common in production. This question tests the ability to reason about ALPN, ACLs, and mesh gateway routing under load, and to propose concrete verification steps and rollback plans.

## Key Concepts
- ALPN negotiation across Consul Connect proxies
- Cross-DC ACL/Intent synchronization
- Envoy sidecar and mesh gateway routing
- TLS certificate rotation and CA trust

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node TLS ALPN probe
const tls = require('tls');
const socket = tls.connect({host:'payments.vm-dc', port:443, ALPNProtocols:['h2','http/1.1']}, () => {
  console.log('ALPN:', socket.alpnProtocol);
  socket.end();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to three DCs with federated tenants?
- How would you instrument traces to differentiate TLS vs application-layer failures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about cross-DC ingress, TLS handshakes, and SNI-driven routing in Consul Connect, plus how to reproduce edge-case failures and rollback safely.

## Key Concepts

- Ingress gateway TLS and SNI routing in Consul Connect
- SANs and wildcard certificate coverage across subdomains
- TLS context synchronization between gateway and sidecars

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js TLS inspection snippet to fetch peer cert for a given host
const https = require('https');
const options = { hostname: 'gateway.example.com', port: 443, servername: 'order.example.com' };
https.get(options, res => {
  console.log(res.socket.getPeerCertificate(true).subject);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you revert a certificate rotation safely in production?
- How would you validate that all existing connections have migrated to the new cert after rollback?
